#
# prima-materia — the clause that puts the effect beyond the bystander, examined
#
# The statement at pmc:TheEffectPutBeyondTheBystander stood at the head of
# queries/untested-generalizations.rq with five witnesses. Its falsifier named
# the shape to look for first, an operative text that prescribes its operation
# be performed before a stranger as a proof, and the corpus to look in first,
# the Siberian material. This protocol puts both to the holding, reads the
# chapter of the first witness again where it gives its examples, and opens
# the Chukchee corpus at the pages on feats performed before others.
#

pmp:EffectPutBeyondTheBystanderExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Whether an operation whose effect a bystander would see always carries a clause withdrawing it from him"@en, "Ob eine Verrichtung, deren Wirkung ein Umstehender sähe, stets eine Klausel trägt, die sie ihm entzieht"@de ;
    skos:definition "A protocol on the claim that a transmission teaching an operation whose effect would be evident to anyone standing by carries in the operative passage a clause putting the effect beyond a bystander's reach, and that where it does not, the outcome is one a stranger can read off and the operation is shown. The population is every tradition in the holding beyond pm:corpusNamed with at least one operative passage, an instruction, a capacity catalogued for an operation or a recorded practice, promising an effect on the material world that a bystander would see when it occurred; narrative of the past, a capacity of the dead or of a figure of the transmission's history, and protective effects whose outcome is an absence are excluded. Drawn on 2026-10-18 on main by a scan of the English labels and definitions of every Yielding, Conceptualizing, Attributing and Practicing node with pm:withinTradition for invisibility, flight, walking on water, ground turned to water, transmutation and gold, rain, storm and lightning, fire kindled or not burning, standing still, not bleeding, passing through walls, shape-changing and apparition, 170 hits; every hit read at the node, and each tradition so found searched again in labels, definitions and notes for secrecy, concealment, the public and publication. Read at the wording for this protocol: the reopened chapter of the Visuddhimagga with the discourse it quotes, and Bogoras on the Chukchee opened further, both named in examinations/searches-effect-put-beyond-the-bystander.ttl."@en ;
    pm:tests pmc:TheEffectPutBeyondTheBystander ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "First, as the statement names it: an operative passage that states bystanders do see the effect, and sharper, an operative text that prescribes the operation be performed before a stranger as a proof. Met, at the Romanus-Büchlein, whose blood-staunching letter is to be tested by whoever will not believe it on an animal that will not bleed (pmc:RomanusProbaAdIncredulum); at the Chukchee contest, where every practitioner present must show his ability and is paid according to it (pmc:ChukcheeContestOfPractitioners); and within the first witness's own chapter, which defines a class of miracle by its being seen together with its performer and gives only public cases of it (pmc:PakataPatihariya). Second, implied by the second clause: an operation of the kind that carries no such clause and is not shown either. Met in four transmissions of seventeen."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:casesWithDeviation ;
    pm:baseRate "Seventeen traditions in the holding on 2026-10-18 carry an operative passage of the kind. Six carry a clause of the statement's kind attached to the operation: Theravāda, Thelema, the outer alchemy of Ge Hong, the Daoist chapter of the same author, Agrippa, and the Chukchee séance. Two carry a rule that publishing the teaching makes it powerless, but attached to the method and not to the performance: Rasaśāstra and the Nāth commentary. Five carry no clause and the outcome is read off by a stranger or shown to one: the European folk handbooks, Latin alchemy with its assays, the Kongo ordeals, the Taulipang jaguar certified by bystanders, and the New England healer's exhibitions. Four carry neither a clause nor a showing: the Solomonic grimoires, the Sword of Moses, the Eddic charms and Jābir's generation of animals. Two of the six, Theravāda and the Chukchee, also show the same class of operation in public, so the six and the five are not disjoint."@en ;
    pm:counterSearch pm:counterSearchFoundOpposition ;
    pm:counterSearchNote "Searched: the compiled holding as of 2026-10-18 on main, by the scan in the definition; read at the node, besides the five witnesses: pmc:RomanusProbaAdIncredulum, pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicKuhFaelltTodt, pmc:AbramelinInvisibilitas, pmc:AbramelinVolatus, pmc:AbramelinAqua, pmc:ClaviculaCeteraeVirtutes, pmc:HarbaDeMoshe125, pmc:HarbaDeMoshe048, pmc:HarbaDeMosheSecrecy, pmc:NorseSalrLogi, pmc:NorseFleinnStodvadr, pmc:JabirTakwinAlHayawan, pmc:RasarnavaIcchayaAdrsyata, pmc:RasaGuptavidya, pmc:NathDivyaVapus, pmc:NathMahamudraGopaniyata, pmc:HedanTiaojian, pmc:JiaqiuWaiwu, pmc:AgrippaSilentium, pmc:AvibhavaTirobhava, pmc:PathaviyaUmmujjanimujja, pmc:AkasenaGamana, pmc:VikubbanaIddhi, pmc:IddhiOccultanda, pmc:DisplayOfPowersRefused, pmc:TaulipangJaguarCertifiedByTheBystanders, pmc:JainaTejolesya, pmc:BouitiDindimbaStopsTheLikoundou, pmc:ChanJianMituo, pmc:TungusMarkedAnimals, pmc:TheOrdealGiverMustStandTheOtherOrdeals, pmc:QuimbyProbaAdAegrum, pmc:SwedenborgSignaNonCoguntFidem. Read at the wording outside the holding: Visuddhimagga XII.69-86 in two translations and the Pali, Saṃyutta Nikāya 41.4 in the Pali and Woodward's English, and Bogoras on the Chukchee, pp. 415-423 and 435-447. Found for the statement: one new witness of the third device in the performance sense, Ge Hong's rule that the elixir be compounded where there are no people and that unbelievers must not know of it (pmc:HedanTiaojian); and one device the statement did not name, the effect shown to a stranger only under conditions that exclude his check, darkness and a ban on touching, at pmc:ChukcheeNoSeparateVoicesByDaylight and pmc:ChukcheeSpiritsNotToBeTouched. Found against it: the falsifier's sharp shape twice, the Theravāda chapter's public examples, and four transmissions with neither clause nor showing. Not searched: the works behind the holding's grimoire and Eddic nodes, so that a clause standing elsewhere in Abramelin, the Key of Solomon, the Sword of Moses or Hávamál and not harvested is not in the count; and the scan is lexical over English wording."@en ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-18" ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-10-18" ;
    skos:note "The first clause fails as a rule and survives as a device. The statement said that where an operation of this kind is taught, the passage carries the clause. Six of seventeen do. The six are not a cluster: Pāli, Chinese twice from one author, Latin, English and a Siberian oral practice, from the fourth century to the twentieth. That is the same result as the protocol at examinations/the-rule-against-reporting-the-absence-tested.ttl, a recurring minority device, and the statement is rewritten at the node to say so, with the old wording kept in a note."@en ;
    skos:note "The second clause fails as a partition, and this is the finding that was not expected. It said that where no clause appears the outcome is one a stranger can read off and the operation is shown, which would have made clause and showing two answers given by two kinds of transmission. In the holding they are given by the same transmissions on different occasions. The Visuddhimagga reserves the changed ground to the operator and, in the same chapter, defines the evident miracle as the one in which power and performer are both seen and gives as its cases a disciple diving into the earth in the middle of an assembly and resolving that the people should see him, and an elder opening the hells to an audience while preaching. The Chukchee keep the séance in the dark and the spirits from a stranger's touch, and require every practitioner at a ceremonial to show his ability in the outer tent and pay him by it; a novice who hides his power is struck by it. What decides between clause and showing is the occasion and, in the Pāli case, the operator's wish, and neither is something a stranger can observe beforehand."@en ;
    skos:note "What the four transmissions with neither clause nor showing have in common, stated because it is the next thing to examine and not a conclusion. All four are written catalogues of operations given in a sentence each — the grimoire chapter, the numbered recipe, the stanza of a charm list, the table of media — and none of them describes an occasion on which the operation is performed. A clause of this kind may be a property of transmissions that describe performances, and the grimoires may carry theirs in the parts of the works the holding did not harvest; both are open."@en ;
    skos:note "The sharpest case is the Chukchee one, and it does not fall cleanly on either side. A practitioner offered to stab himself on the bare body before the recorder and prepared it to the last drumming; a voice of the performance then forbade all feats until a reindeer owed to a spirit was sacrificed, which the season put off for months, and he showed his scars instead (pmc:ChukcheeProofForbiddenByASpirit). The proof was offered and the bystander was present. What withdrew it was not a clause in the operation but an occasion produced inside the performance, which is the third answer to the same pressure that the statement's note on its neighbours describes, moved from after the session to just before it."@en ;
    skos:note "A case from the falsifier's own list that turned the other way. The statement named the Siberian and Inuit material as the place where a feat performed before a gathering to convince people would most likely be found. The Chukchee corpus has it, and has it alongside the darkness, the ban on touching and the reduced performance before strangers (pmc:ChukcheeShamanShunsStrangers). The recorder adds that the feats in the light are common enough that spectators give them little weight, which is the one statement in the material read about what showing does to an audience, and it agrees with the reason the Vinaya gives for forbidding display."@en .
